\]\n\n   Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n5. **Existence**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) has \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). All lines are non\u2011sunny, distinct, and the covering condition holds.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   These cover all points of \\(S\\) except \\((1,n)\\). Add a sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and any other point of \\(S\\); for instance the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (since \\(n\\ge3\\)), so it is sunny. The resulting set consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nBoth constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N=|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximal coverage by non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor a non\u2011sunny line we have three families:\n\n* horizontal line \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);\n* vertical line \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\);\n* diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe numbers of points of \\(S\\) lying on these lines are  \n\n\\[\n|S\\cap\\{y=b\\}| = n+1-b,\\qquad\n|S\\cap\\{x=a\\}| = n+1-a,\\qquad\n|S\\cap\\{x+y=c\\}| = c-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\), and the possible sizes are exactly  \n\n\\[\nn,n-1,\\dots ,1 .\n\\]\n\nIf several non\u2011sunny lines are chosen, points belonging to the intersection of two chosen lines are counted only once.  \nTo maximise the number of *distinct* points covered we must avoid intersections.  \nAny diagonal line intersects every horizontal line, and a vertical line intersects every horizontal line as well; therefore a collection of only horizontal lines yields the largest possible total.\n\nConsequently, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nC(t)=\\max\\bigl\\{|\\,\\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}L_i\\cap S\\,|\\;:\\;L_i\\text{ non\u2011sunny}\\bigr\\}\n      =\\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}(n-i)\n      =t\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t-1}{2}\\Bigr)\n      =t\\Bigl(n+1-\\frac{t+1}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessary condition for a given number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny and the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so the points not covered by the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines are at most \\(k\\) (each can be covered by a distinct sunny line). Hence  \n\n\\[\nN-C(t)\\le k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(k=n-t\\) and \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), inequality (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nC(t)-t\\ge N-n\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nt\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t+1}{2}\\Bigr)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expression (1) for \\(C(t)\\) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t+1}{2}\\Bigr)&\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\\\\n2tn-t(t+1)&\\ge n^{2}-n\\\\\n0&\\ge n^{2}-n-2tn+t^{2}+t\\\\\n0&\\ge (t-n)^{2}+(t-n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(u=t-n\\). The last inequality is \\(u^{2}+u\\le0\\), i.e. \\(-1\\le u\\le0\\).  \nSince \\(u\\) is an integer, the only possibilities are \\(u=0\\) or \\(u=-1\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nt=n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad t=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nRecalling that \\(k=n-t\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can exist**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions showing that \\(k=0,1\\) are attainable\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) has \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). All lines are non\u2011sunny, distinct, and the covering condition holds.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  These cover every point of \\(S\\) except \\((1,n)\\) (the only point with \\(b=n\\)).  \n  Add a sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and any other point of \\(S\\); for instance the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\). Its slope is  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{1-n}{2-1}=1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n  \\]\n\n  so the line is sunny. The set now consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nBoth constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (4) shows that only \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) can occur, and the explicit constructions above demonstrate that both values are indeed attainable. Hence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
