al.\n\nConsequently, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nC(t)=\\max\\bigl\\{|\\,\\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}L_i\\cap S\\,|\\;:\\;L_i\\text{ non‑sunny}\\bigr\\}\n      =\\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}(n-i)\n      =t\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t-1}{2}\\Bigr)\n      =t\\Bigl(n+1-\\frac{t+1}{2}\\Bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessary condition for a given number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny and the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  \nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so the points not covered by the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines are at most \\(k\\) (each can be covered by a distinct sunny line). Hence  \n\n\\[\nN-C(t)\\le k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(k=n-t\\) and \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), inequality (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nC(t)-t\\ge N-n\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nt\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t+1}{2}\\Bigr)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expression (1) for \\(C(t)\\) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt\\Bigl(n-\\frac{t+1}{2}\\Bigr)&\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\\\\n2tn-t(t+1)&\\ge n^{2}-n\\\\\n0&\\ge n^{2}-n-2tn+t^{2}+t\\\\\n0&\\ge (t-n)^{2}+(t-n).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(u=t-n\\). The last inequality is \\(u^{2}+u\\le0\\), i.e. \\(-1\\le u\\le0\\).  \nSince \\(u\\) is an integer, the only possibilities are \\(u=0\\) or \\(u=-1\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nt=n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad t=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nRecalling that \\(k=n-t\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can exist**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions showing that \\(k=0,1\\) are attainable\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) has \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). All lines are non‑sunny, distinct, and the covering condition 