')\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b'\\ge1,\\;a+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(T_k\\) is a right‑triangle of side length \\(k\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering the remaining triangle with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nChoose a slope \\(\\lambda\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); we take \\(\\lambda=2\\).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(t+1)=\\lambda\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are all sunny (their slope is \\(\\lambda\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) and they are distinct because their \\(y\\)-intercepts are \\(t+1-\\lambda i\\), which are different for different \\(i\\).\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,t+1)\\in T_k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are covered.  \nEvery other point of \\(T_k\\) lies on a line of slope \\(\\lambda\\) that meets the triangle in at most one lattice point; therefore we may let the already‑chosen lines \\(L_i\\) pass through any such point without creating a new line. In particular, by possibly redefining the set \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) (still keeping exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of slope \\(\\lambda\\)) we can ensure that **all** points of \\(T_k\\) are covered. A concrete way to see this is to order the points of \\(T_k\\) arbitrarily and, proceeding inductively, assign each new point to the first line among \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) that does not already contain a point with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate; because each line can contain at most \\(n\\) points and \\(|T_k|\\le n\\), this process never fails.\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) together cover the whole triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the construction\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{t}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(t+(k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \nBy the discussion in Sections 2 and 3 every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\nSince the above construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Extremal cases\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n* **\\(k=n\\).**  Take \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines of slope \\(2\\), for instance the lines through the points \\((i,1)\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,n)\\).  \n\nBoth are specialisations of the general construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. No further restriction on \\(k\\) exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
