oth coordinates are at least \\(1\\) and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe number of such points equals \\(t_{\\max}+1\\).  \nFrom the inequalities \\(1+qt\\le n\\) and \\(n-1-p\\,t\\ge1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\max}\\le\\min\\!\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                         \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{p}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\#(\\ell\\cap S_n)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n                 \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (3) can occur only when \\(q=2\\) or \\(p=2\\).  \nThus a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of points covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\), we need  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor = n/2\\), and (4) becomes \\(n^2/2 \\ge n(n+1)/2\\), i.e. \\(n\\ge n+1\\), impossible.  \nThus for even \\(n\\ge4\\) all lines cannot be sunny.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  \nThen \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor = m+1\\), and (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nn(m+1)= (2m+1)(m+1) \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2\n      \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; m+1 \\;\\ge\\; m+1 ,\n\\]\n\nso equality holds.  \nHence each sunny line must contain exactly \\(m+1=(n+1)/2\\) points.  \nFrom the discussion above this is possible only for slopes \\(-1/2\\) or \\(-2\\).  \nConsequently at most two such lines can exist, contradicting \\(n\\ge5\\).  \nThus for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) all lines cannot be sunny either.\n\nTherefore **for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final characterization\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) we have the admissible values \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n  The case \\(k=3\\) is realised by the three sunny lines described in §1.  \n* For \\(n\\ge4\\) the only admissible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realised by the constructions in §1.  \n* The values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k>n\\) are impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\) by the arguments of §2 and §3.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\qquad\n       \\text{for }n\\ge4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}.}\n\\]"
