            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0,\\;1\\) and for \\(k=3\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n\\), hence lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \n  None of these lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n  The only point of \\(S_n\\) not covered by these lines is \\((1,n)\\).  \n  The line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{n-1}{1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \n  Thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) (only for \\(n=3\\)).**  \n  For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n  \\]\n\n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;(1,2)\\text{–}(3,1),\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;(1,3)\\text{–}(2,1),\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\;(2,2)\\text{–}(2,2)\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, each is sunny, and together they cover all six points.  \n  Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nColour a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) **black** if \\(a+b\\) is even and **white** otherwise.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nc(\\ell)=\\#\\{\\text{black points of }S_n\\text{ on }\\ell\\}\n        -\\#\\{\\text{white points of }S_n\\text{ on }\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal or vertical, the points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) alternate in colour, so \\(|c(\\ell)|\\le1\\).  \n* If \\(\\ell\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\), all its points have the same colour, so \\(|c(\\ell)|\\) equals the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) (at most \\(n\\)).  \n* If \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, the same two possibilities occur, hence \\(|c(\\ell)|\\le n\\).\n\nLet a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exist.  \nDenote the sunny lines by \\(L_1,L_2\\) and the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nAt most one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) can be a diagonal (otherwise two diagonals would be parallel), so at most one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) can contribute more than \\(1\\) to the colour‑imbalance; all the other \\(n-3\\) lines contribute at most \\(1\\) each.\n\nThe total colour‑imbalance of all points of \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\nD_n:=\\#\\{\\text{black points}\\}-\\#\\{\\text{white points}\\}\n     =\\begin{cases}\n        m & (n=2m),\\\\[2pt]\n        m+1 & (n=2m+1).\n      \\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contributions of all lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n|c(L_1)+c(L_2)|\n   + (n-3)\\cdot1\n   + n\n   \\;\\ge\\;|D_n|.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2m\\) we have \\(|D_n|=m\\) and the left–hand side of (2) is at least  \n\\(2\\cdot1+(2m-3)\\cdot1+m=3m-2>m\\).  \nFor \\(n=2m+1\\) we have \\(|D_n|=m+1\\) and the left–hand side is at least  \n\\(2\\cdot1+(2m-2)\\cdot1+(2m+1)=4m+1>m+1\\).  \nThus (2) can never hold, a contradiction.  \nHence **no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  \nWrite its slope as \\(-p/q\\) in lowest terms, where \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb N\\) and \\(q\\ge2\\).  \nThe integer points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(1+qt,\\;n-1-p\\,t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,t_{\\max},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t_{\\max}\\) is the largest integer for which both coordinates are at least \\(1\\) and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe number of such points equals \\(t_{\\max}+1\\).  \nFrom the inequalities \\(1+qt\\le n\\) and \\(n-1-p\\,t\\ge1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\max}\\le\\min\\!\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                         \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{p}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\#(\\ell\\cap S_n)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n                 \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (3) can occur only when \\(q=2\\) or \\(p=2\\).  \nThus a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of points covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\), we need  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor = n/2\\), and (4) becomes \\(n^2/2 \\ge n(n+1)/2\\), i.e. \\(n\\ge n+1\\), impossible.  \nThus for even \\(n\\ge4\\) all lines cannot be sunny.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  \nThen \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor = m+1\\), and (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nn(m+1)= (2m+1)(m+1) \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2\n      \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; m+1 \\;\\ge\\; m+1 ,\n\\]\n\nso equality holds.  \nHence each sunny line must contain exactly \\(m+1=(n+1)/2\\) points.  \nFrom the discussion above this is possible only for slopes \\(-1/2\\) or \\(-2\\).  \nConsequently at most two such lines can exist, contradicting \\(n\\ge5\\).  \nThus for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) all lines cannot be sunny either.\n\nTherefore **for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final characterization\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) we have the admissible values \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n  The case \\(k=3\\) is realised by the three sunny lines described in §1.  \n* For \\(n\\ge4\\) the only admissible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realised by the constructions in §1.  \n* The values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k>n\\) are impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\) by the arguments of §2 and §3.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\qquad\n       \\text{for }n\\ge4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
