            "role": "user",
            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \n  The only point of \\(S_n\\) not covered is \\((1,n)\\).  \n  The line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-\\,(n-1)\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and distinct from the horizontal lines.  \n  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).**  \n  \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;(1,2)-(3,1)\\;(slope=-\\tfrac12),\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;(1,3)-(2,1)\\;(slope=-2),\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\;(1,1)-(2,2)\\;(slope=1)\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct and together contain all six points.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write its slope as \\(-p/q\\) with coprime positive integers \\(p,q\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,1)\\) (the excluded case corresponds to slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll integer points of \\(\\ell\\) have the form  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+qt,\\;b_0-pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nfor some particular solution \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\nFor points of \\(S_n\\) we need \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nFrom (2.1),\n\n\\[\na+b=(a_0+b_0)+(q-p)t .\n\\]\n\nSince \\((p,q)\\neq(1,1)\\) we have \\(|q-p|\\ge1\\) and, more importantly,\n\n\\[\np+q\\ge3 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIncreasing \\(t\\) by \\(1\\) raises \\(a\\) by \\(q\\) and lowers \\(b\\) by \\(p\\); consequently the quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(-(p-q)\\) and the *distance* from the line \\(a+b=n+1\\) decreases by at least \\(p+q\\ge3\\) each step.  \nHence along \\(\\ell\\) the values of \\(a+b\\) that are \\(\\le n+1\\) can occur for at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1- (a_0+b_0)}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent integers \\(t\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nfor every sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points covered by non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nFor \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) the numbers of points of \\(S_n\\) on the distinct horizontal (or vertical) lines are  \n\n\\[\nn,\\;n-1,\\;n-2,\\dots ,1 .\n\\]\n\nFor the diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) the numbers are  \n\n\\[\n1,2,3,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if we take any \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that they can cover is at most the sum of the \\(m\\) largest numbers among the above sequences, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\max_{\\,\\text{distinct non‑sunny }L_1,\\dots ,L_m}\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^m (L_i\\cap S_n)\\Bigr|\n\\le\n\\frac{m\\bigl(2n-m+1\\bigr)}2 .\n\\;}\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained by choosing the \\(m\\) longest horizontal (or vertical) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  No configuration with two sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nLet the two sunny lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) and let the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \nUsing (2.3) and (3.1) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points that can be covered:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} (L\\cap S_n)\\bigr|\n&\\le\n|\\ell_1\\cap S_n|+|\\ell_2\\cap S_n|\n   +\\frac{(n-2)\\bigl(2n-(n-2)+1\\bigr)}2 \\\\[2mm]\n&\\le\n2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   +\\frac{(n-2)(n+3)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward calculation shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\n2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   +\\frac{(n-2)(n+3)}2\n   \\;<\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |S_n|.\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered by a family containing exactly two sunny lines.  \nHence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\)**, and in particular no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  No configuration with all lines sunny for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nSuppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBy (2.3) each line contains at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^n (\\ell_i\\cap S_n)\\bigr|\n\\le\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(n=2t\\) and the right–hand side of (5.1) equals \\(t\\cdot 2t = n^2/2\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  Hence an all‑sunny configuration cannot exist.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  Then (5.1) gives the exact equality  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=|S_n|.\n\\]\n\nConsequently every sunny line would have to attain the maximal size \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}=m+1\\).  \nEquality in (2.3) occurs only when \\(p+q=3\\); i.e. the slope is \\(-2\\) (\\(p=2,q=1\\)) or \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (\\(p=1,q=2\\)).  \nAt most two distinct lines can have one of these two slopes, so for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) we cannot have \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n\nThus **all‑sunny configurations are impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points.  \nThe three sunny lines displayed in §1 cover all of them, giving \\(k=3\\).  \nThe constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) also work, while the argument of §4 excludes \\(k=2\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final classification\n\nCombining the explicit constructions with the impossibility results we obtain:\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.\n* For \\(n=3\\) the additional value \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n* No other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3:\\;k=0,1,3;\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge4:\\;k=0,1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
