y lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{horizontal: } y=c,\\\\\n&\\text{vertical:   } x=c,\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal:   } x+y=c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor distinct horizontal (or vertical) lines the numbers of points of\n\\(S_n\\) are \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,1\\); for distinct diagonal lines they are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  Therefore any collection of \\(m\\) distinct\nnon‑sunny lines covers at most the sum of the \\(m\\) largest numbers among\nthese sequences, namely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{m\\bigl(2n-m+1\\bigr)}2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained by taking the \\(m\\) longest horizontal (or vertical)\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  No configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume a covering consisting of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, with \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\).\nUsing (2.5) for each sunny line and (3.1) for the non‑sunny ones we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}(L\\cap S_n)\\bigr|\n\\le\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n+\\frac{(n-k)\\bigl(2n-(n-k)+1\\bigr)}2\n=:T_n(k). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward algebraic check shows that for every integer\n\\(n\\ge4\\) and every \\(k\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\nT_n(k)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (4.2).*  \nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case* \\(n=2m\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and\n\n\\[\nT_n(k)=km+\\frac{(2m-k)(2m+k+1)}2\n      =m(2m+1)-\\frac{(k-1)(m-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\ge2\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\), the subtracted term is positive, whence\n\\(T_n(k)<m(2m+1)=|S_n|\\).\n\n*Odd case* \\(n=2m+1\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and\n\n\\[\nT_n(k)=(k)(m+1)+\\frac{(2m+1-k)(2m+1+k+1)}2\n      =(2m+1)(m+1)-\\frac{(k-1)m}2 .\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(m\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\) give a strictly positive subtraction,\nso \\(T_n(k)<(2m+1)(m+