floor)\\).  Counting the integer points gives\n\\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) exactly when \\(c=n+1\\); this yields the line\n\\(y=-2x+(n+1)=L_2\\).\n\n*Case \\((p,q)=(-1,2)\\).*  \nNow (7) is \\(2y=-x+r\\) or \\(y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{r}{2}\\).  Write\n\\(r=2c\\) (otherwise the line would not contain integer points).  Then\n\\(y=-\\frac{x}{2}+c\\).  The smallest admissible point is \\((2,c-1)\\) and\n\\(c\\ge2\\).  The largest point inside the triangle is\n\\((2\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor,c-\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor)\\).  The number of\ninteger points equals \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) precisely\nwhen \\(c=\\frac{n+2}{2}\\); this gives the line\n\\(y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{n+2}{2}=L_3\\).\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) the only lines attaining the bound are the three\nlisted in (6).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n1. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n\n2. **\\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and together with the vertical lines covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **\\(k=3\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The six points that remain uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1). \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_a&:\\;(n-2,1)\\;(n-1,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n   L_b&:\\;(n-2,2)\\;(n,1)   &&\\text{slope }-\\frac12,\\\\\n   L_c&:\\;(n-2,3)\\;(n-1,1) &&\\text{slope }-2,\n   \\end{aligned} \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   cover exactly the six points in (15).  Hence a configuration with\n   exactly three sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nLet \\(V\\) be the set of vertical (non‑sunny) lines among the remaining\n\\(n-2\\) lines.\n\n*Case 1: \\(|V|\\le n-3\\).*  \nThen at least three columns, say \\(a=n-2,n-1,n\\), are **not** covered by a\nvertical line.  In each of these columns a sunny line can contain at most\none point (otherwise two points of the same column would have the same\n\\(a\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical).  Consequently the two\nsunny lines together can cover at most two points in each of the three\ncolumns, i.e. at most six points altogether.\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) that lie in those three columns are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(n-2,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le 3\\}\\cup\\{(n-1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le 2\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nsix points in total.  If any of the non‑sunny lines is not vertical, it is\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); such a line meets each of the three\ncolumns in **at most one** point, so it cannot increase the total number\nof covered points beyond six.  Hence all six points would have to be\ncovered by the two sunny lines alone, which is impossible because a line\nof slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can contain at most three of the\nsix points (a direct check of the six possible pairs shows that any line\nthrough two of them misses at least one of the remaining four).  Thus\nthis case cannot occur.\n\n*Case 2: \\(|V| = n-2\\) (all non‑sunny lines are vertical).*  \nThen exactly two columns are left uncovered; without loss of generality\nthey are the last two columns \\(a=n-1\\) and \\(a=n\\).  The points that must\nbe covered by the two sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(n-1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le 2\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nthree points.  Any sunny line can contain at most two of them (the three\npoints are not collinear with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\nConsequently the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points,\nbut we need to cover **all three** points; this would force one of the\nsunny lines to contain exactly two of them and the other to contain the\nremaining one.  However, the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) lie on a\nvertical line, which is forbidden for a sunny line; similarly any line\nthrough \\((n,1)\\) and one of the points in column \\(n-1\\) has slope\n\\(-1\\) or \\(0\\).  Hence no sunny line can pass through two of the three\nrequired points, a contradiction.\n\nBoth cases lead to contradictions; therefore a configuration with\nexactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\nAmong the \\(n\\) lines there are \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny ones.  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Claim.*  We may assume that all \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical.  \n\nIndeed, replace every horizontal or slope‑\\(-1\\) non‑sunny line by a\nvertical line that passes through any point of that line which lies in\n\\(S_n\\).  Such a replacement can only **increase** the set of points that\nare already covered by non‑sunny lines, and therefore can only **decrease**\nthe set of points that must be covered by the sunny lines.  Hence if a\ncovering exists with some non‑vertical non‑sunny lines, it also exists after\nthe replacement, and we may work with the vertical choice.\n\nConsequently the uncovered points form exactly the triangle  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}, \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Even \\(k\\)\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, Lemma 1 applied to the triangle \\(S_k\\) (the proof of\nLemma 1 uses only the bound \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) and therefore is valid for any\nsuch triangle) gives  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_k|\\le\\frac{k}{2}\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }L. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nWith \\(k\\) sunny lines the total number of points that can be covered is\nat most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2 > \\dfrac{k^{2}}2\\) for every even \\(k\\ge4\\),\na contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  \nLemma 1 tells us that any sunny line meets \\(S_k\\) in at most \\(m+1\\)\npoints, and equality can occur only for the three lines listed in (6)\nwith \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:y=x,\\\\\nL_2&:y=-2x+(k+1),\\\\\nL_3&:y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{k+2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThus at most three sunny lines can contain the maximal number \\(m+1\\) of\npoints of \\(S_k\\); every other sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points.\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n3(m+1)+(k-3)m\n=3(m+1)+(2m+1-3)m\n=km+m-2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2 = (2m+1)(m+1)\\), we compare:\n\n\\[\nkm+m-2 = (2m+1)m+m-2 = 2m^{2}+2m-2\n\\]\n\\[\n|S_k| = (2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(m\\ge2\\) we have \\(2m^{2}+2m-2 < 2m^{2}+3m+1\\); therefore the sunny\nlines cannot cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  This contradiction shows that\nno configuration exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) with the impossibility\nresults for all other non‑negative integers, we obtain the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]"
