                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** when its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (size of a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nMoreover at most three sunny lines attain this bound; they are the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:y=x,\\\\[2pt]\nL_2&:y=-2x+(n+1),\\\\[2pt]\nL_3&:y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{n+2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(n\\) is odd the second family contains one point fewer, so only the first and third families actually attain the bound; nevertheless the statement “at most three” remains true.)\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the bipartite graph  \n\n\\[\nG_n=(A,B,E),\\qquad \nA=B=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((i,j)\\in E\\) iff \\(i+j\\le n+1\\).  \nThe set \\(S_n\\) is precisely the edge set of \\(G_n\\).\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is not vertical, its \\(a\\)-coordinates on \\(L\\) are all distinct; if it is not horizontal, its \\(b\\)-coordinates are all distinct.  Hence the set  \n\n\\[\nM:=L\\cap S_n\n\\]\n\nis a **matching** in \\(G_n\\) (no two edges of \\(M\\) share a vertex on the left or on the right).\n\nA matching of size \\(t\\) can be written as \\(\\{(a_i,b_i)\\}_{i=1}^t\\) with\n\\(a_1<a_2<\\dots<a_t\\).  Because each edge satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nb_i\\le n+1-a_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSumming (4) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^t (a_i+b_i)\\le t(n+1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand \\(a_i\\ge i\\) and \\(b_i\\ge i\\) (the \\(i\\)-th smallest left‑ and right‑vertices are at least \\(i\\)), so  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^t (a_i+b_i)\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^t (i+i)=t(t+1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) yields \\(t(t+1)\\le t(n+1)\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is an integer, (2) follows.\n\nFor equality we must have equality in both (5) and (6).  Equality in (6) forces  \n\n\\[\na_i=i,\\qquad b_i=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the matching consists of the edges \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\).  Because \\(t=\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\), the points \\((i,i)\\) lie on the line \\(y=x\\); this gives the first family in (3).\n\nIf the matching contains an edge \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\), then (4) forces \\(j=n+1-i\\).  Consequently the matching consists of the edges  \n\n\\[\n(i,n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, (8) yields the line \\(y=-2x+(n+1)\\); when \\(n\\) is odd the last edge would be \\((\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2})\\), which already belongs to the first family, so the second family attains the bound only for even \\(n\\).\n\nA third possibility occurs when the left‑vertices are the odd numbers \\(1,3,5,\\dots\\).  Writing \\(a_i=2i-1\\) and using (4) gives \\(b_i=n+2-i\\); the points \\((2i-1,n+2-i)\\) lie on the line \\(y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{n+2}{2}\\), which is the third family in (3).  This family always attains the bound.\n\nThus any sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and at most three sunny lines achieve this maximum. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n1. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n\n2. **\\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\d