b\\)-coordinate.  Hence each sunny line can cover at most three points of \\(P\\), and the two sunny lines together can cover at most six points of \\(P\\).\n\nSince \\(|N|=n-2\\) and we have used at most \\(n-3\\) vertical lines, there is exactly one non‑sunny line left; it is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=d\\)).  Its intersection with \\(P\\) is easily listed:\n\n* **Horizontal.**  \n  \\(c=1\\) gives the three points \\((n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1)\\);  \n  \\(c=2\\) gives \\((n-2,2),(n-1,2)\\);  \n  \\(c=3\\) gives \\((n-2,3)\\).  \n  In any case the horizontal line contributes at most three points of \\(P\\).\n\n* **Diagonal.**  \n  The possible sums \\(a+b\\) of points in \\(P\\) are \\(n-1,n,n+1\\).  \n  For \\(d=n-1\\) we obtain \\((n-2,1)\\);  \n  for \\(d=n\\) we obtain \\((n-2,2),(n-1,1)\\);  \n  for \\(d=n+1\\) we obtain \\((n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\).  \n  Again at most three points of \\(P\\) are covered.\n\nThus the non‑sunny line covers at most three points of \\(P\\).  Observe that the three points of \\(P\\) with \\(b=1\\) are \\((n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1)\\).  A sunny line cannot be horizontal, so it can contain **at most one** of these three points.  Consequently at least one of them must be covered by the non‑sunny line, which forces the non‑sunny line to be either \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(y=1\\)*, it already covers the three points with \\(b=1\\).  The remaining points of \\(P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nQ=\\{(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line can contain at most one point with \\(b=2\\); therefore at most two points of \\(Q\\) can be covered by the two sunny lines.  The third point of \\(Q\\) stays uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(x+y=n+1\\)*, it again covers the three points with \\(b=1\\) and leaves the same set \\(Q\\) uncovered, and the same argument applies.\n\nHence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Therefore \\(k=2\\