\n#### Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nLet the non‑sunny lines be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); denote by\n\\(V\\) the set of vertical lines among them.\n\n*Case 1 – \\(|V|\\le n-3\\).*  \nThen at least three columns, say \\(a=n-2,n-1,n\\), are not covered by a\nvertical line.  In each of these columns a sunny line can contain **at most one**\npoint (otherwise the line would be vertical).  Consequently the two sunny\nlines together can cover at most two points in each of the three columns,\ni.e. at most six points altogether.\n\nThe remaining non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or diagonal\n(\\(x+y=c\\)).  A direct inspection of the six points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(n-2,i)\\mid i=1,2,3\\}\\cup\\{(n-1,i)\\mid i=1,2\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nshows that a horizontal line can meet at most three of them (it can meet at\nmost one point in each column), and a diagonal line can also meet at most three\npoints (its equation forces \\(a+b=c\\), so at most one point per column).  Hence\nthe three non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\(6+3=9\\) points, whereas\nthe set (11) contains **ten** points when \\(n\\ge4\\).  Thus at least one point\nremains uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n*Case 2 – \\(|V|=n-2\\).*  \nThen all columns except possibly the last one are covered by vertical lines.\nThe uncovered column is \\(a=n\\); it contains only the point \\((n,1)\\).\nBoth sunny lines must pass through this point, which would make them parallel,\ncontradicting the requirement that they be distinct.  Hence this case is also\nimpossible.\n\nTherefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\nTake \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines; the points that are not covered by\nthem form the smaller triangle  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining lines are sunny, so they must cover every point of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Even \\(k\\).*  \nLemma 1 applied with \\(n=k\\) gives \\(|L\\cap S_k|\\le k/2\\) for each sunny\nline \\(L\\).  Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2<\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|S_k|\\) for every even \\(k\\ge4\\),\nthe points of \\(S_k\\) cannot all be covered – a contradiction.\n\n*Odd \\(k\\ge5\\).*  \nNow \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\).  Lemma 1 tells us\nthat a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_k\\), and\nequality can occur **only** for the three lines listed in (3) (with \\(n\\) replaced\nby \\(k\\)).  Consequently at most three of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can attain the\nmaximum size; every other sunny line contains at most \\(\\frac{k-1}{2}\\) points.\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}+(k-3)\\cdot\\frac{k-1}{2}\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2-1 < |S_k|,\n\\]\n\nwhich again contradicts the requirement that all points of \\(S_k\\) be covered.\nThus no configuration exists for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\nCombining the two sub‑cases, no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\) and proved that no\nconfiguration exists for any other non‑negative integer \\(k\\).\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]"
