"### Summary ###\n\nSolution found with answer: \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution, but I have rigorously proven that the only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) lines exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there are configurations with exactly \\(0\\), \\(1\\) or \\(3\\) sunny lines, and no configuration exists with any other number of sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Maximum size of a sunny line (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   For a line \\(L\\) not parallel to the three forbidden directions, the set \\(L\\cap S_n\\) is a matching in the bipartite graph with parts \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the \\(a\\)\u2013coordinates) and \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the \\(b\\)\u2013coordinates).  \n   One shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, when \\(n\\) is odd equality in (1) can occur only for the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&:y=x,\\\\\n   L_2&:y=-2x+(n+1),\\\\\n   L_3&:y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{n+2}{2},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   each of which contains exactly \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non\u2011sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add a sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical