three sunny lines can achieve the maximal size; any further sunny line would contain fewer than \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points, and the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines would be\n\n   \\[\n   3\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}+(k-3)\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   < k\\cdot\\frac{k+1}{2}=|S_k|,\n   \\]\n\n   again a contradiction.  Hence no configuration exists for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n   Therefore no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) with the impossibility results for all other \\(k\\) yields the final answer.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, equality in (5) can occur only for the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:y=x,\\\\\nL_2&:y=-2x+(n+1),\\\\\nL_3&:y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{n+2}{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\neach of which contains exactly \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(qy=px+r\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\).  \nAll integer points on \\(L\\) are of the shape  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+qt,\\;b_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is a particular integer solution of \\(qy=px+r\\).  \nCondition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+b_0)+t(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal or vertical) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), the integer \\(p+q\\) is non\u2011zero.  \nFrom (8) we obtain a bound on the number of admissible integers \\(t\\); a short computation yields (5).  \n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\).  Then \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor=m+1\\).  \nEquality in (5) forces the interval of admissible \\(t\\) in (8) to have length exactly \\(m\\).  Solving the Diophantine conditions shows that this can happen only when the line meets two sides of the triangle \\(S_n\\) at the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(m+1,m+1) \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n(1,m+1),\\;(m+1,1) \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n(1,m+2),\\;(m+1,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich are precisely the three lines listed in (6).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\n1. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non\u2011sunny and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n\n2. **\\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and together with the vertical lines covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **\\(k=3\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The remaining six points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\\\\n   &(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n   &(n,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_a&:\\;(n-2,1)\\;(n-1,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n   L_b&:\\;(n-2,2)\\;(n,1)   &&\\text{slope }-\\frac12,\\\\\n   L_c&:\\;(n-2,3)\\;(n-1,1) &&\\text{slope }-2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   cover the six points of (10).  Hence a configuration with exactly three sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------